together eager for battle on the holy plain of Kurukshetra, O Sanjaya.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.2',0,'1.2. Sanjaya said Having seen the army of the Pandavas drawn up in battle-array,
King Duryodhana then approached his teacher (Drona) and spoke these words.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.3',0,'1.3. "Behold, O Teacher! this mighty army of the sons of Pandu,
arrayed by the son of Drupada, thy wise disciple.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.4',0,'1.4. Here are heroes, mighty archers, eal in battle to Bhima
and Arjuna, Yoyudhana (Satyaki), Virata and Drupada, of the great car (mighty
warriors).'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.5',0,'1.5. "Dhrishtaketu, chekitana and the valiant king of Kasi, Purujit
and Kuntibhoja and Saibya, the best men.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.6',0,'1.6. "The strong Yodhamanyu and the brave Uttamaujas, the son
of Subhadra (Abhimanyu, the son of Subhadra and Arjuna), and the sons of
Draupadi, all of great chariots (great heroes).'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.7',0,'1.7. "Know also, O best among the twice-born! the names of those
who are the most distinguished amongst ourselves, the leaders of my army;
these I name to thee for thy information.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.8',0,'1.8. "Thyself and Bhishma, and Karna and also Kripa, the victorious
in war, Asvatthama, Vikarna, and also Bhurisrava, the son of Somadatta.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.9',0,'1.9. "And also many other heroes who are ready to give up their
lives for my sake, armed with various weapons and missiles, all well-skilled
in battle.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.10',0,'1.10. "This army of ours marshalled by Bhishma is insufficient,
whereas that army of theirs marshelled by Bhima is sufficient.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.11',0,'1.11. "Therefore do ye all, stationed in your respective positions,
in the several divisions of the army, protect Bhishma alone."'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.12',0,'1.12. His glorious grandsire (Bhishma), the oldest of the Kauravas,
in order to cheer Duryodhana, now roared like a lion, and blew his conch.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.13',0,'1.13. Then (following Bhishma), conches and kettledrums, tabors,
drums and cow horns blared forth ite suddenly (from the Kaurava side)
and the sound was tremendous.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.14',0,'1.14. Then, also, Madhava (Krishna) and the son of Pandu (Arjuna),
seated in the magnificent chariot, yoked with white horses, blew divine
conches.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.15',0,'1.15. Hrishikesha blew the Panchajanya and Arjuna blew the Devadatta
and Bhima (the wolf-bellied), the doer of terrible deeds, blew the great
conch Paundra.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.16',0,'1.16. The king Yodhishthira, the son of Kunti, blew the Anantavijaya;
Nakula and Sahadeva blew the Sughosha and the Manipushpaka.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.17',0,'1.17. The king of Kasi, an exellent archer, Sikhandi, the mighty
car-warrior, Dhrishtadyumna and Virata and Satyaki, the unconered.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.18',0,'1.18. Drupada and the sons of Draupadi, O Lord of the earth, and
the son of Subhadra, the mighty-armed, blew their conches separately.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.19',0,'1.19. That tumultuous sound rent the hearts of (the members of)
Dhritarashtra''s party, making both the heaven and the earth resound.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.20',0,'1.20. Then, seeing the people of Dhritarashtra’s party standing
arrayed and the discharge of weapons about to begin, Arjuna, the son of
Pandu, whose ensign was a monkey, took up his bow and said the following
to Krishna, O Lord of the earth.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.21',0,'1.21 Arjuna said In the middle between the two armies, place my chariot,
O krishna, so that I may behold those who stand here desirous to fight,
and know with whom I must fight, when the battle is about to commence.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.22',0,'1.22. Arjuna said In the middle between the two armies, place my chariot,
O krishna, so that I may behold those who stand here desirous to fight,
and know with whom I must fight, when the battle is about to commence.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.23',0,'1.23. For I desire to observe those who are assembled here to fight,
wishing to please in battle the evil-minded Duryodhana (the son of Dhritarashtra).'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.24',0,'1.24. Sanjaya said Thus addressed by Arjuna, Krishna, having stationed that
best of chariots, O Dhritarashtra, in the midst of the two armies.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.25',0,'1.25. In front of Bhishma and Drona, and all the rulers of the
earth, said: "O Arjuna (son of Pritha), behold these Kurus gathered together."'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.26',0,'1.26. Then, Arjuna (son of Pritha) saw there (in the armies) stationed,
fathers and grandfathers, teachers, maternal uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons
and friends too.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.27',0,'1.27. (He saw) fathers-in-law and friends also in both the armies.
The son of Kunti, Arjuna, seeing all those kinsmen thus standing arrayed,
spoke this, sorrowfully filled with deep pity.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.28',0,'1.28. Arjuna said Seeing these, my kinsmen, O krishna, arrayed, eager to fight.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.29',0,'1.29. My limbs fail and my mouth is parched, my body ivers and
my hair stands on end.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.30',0,'1.30. The (bow) Gandiva slips from my hand, and also my skins burns
all over; I am unable even to stand and my mind is reeling, as it were.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.31',0,'1.31. And I see adverse omens, O Kesava. I do not see any good
in killing my kinsmen in battle.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.32',0,'1.32. I desire not victory, O Krishna, nor kingdom, nor pleasures.
Of what avail is dominion to us, O Krishna, or pleasures or even life?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.33',0,'1.33. Those for whose sake we desire kingdom, enjoyments and pleasures,
stand here in battle, having renounced life and wealth.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.34',0,'1.34. Teachers, fathers, sons and also grandfathers, maternal uncles,
fathers-in-law, grandsons, brothers-in-law and other relatives,-'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.35',0,'1.35. These I do not wish to kill, though they kill me, O Krishna,
even for the sake of dominion over the three worlds; leave alone killing
them for the sake of the earth.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.36',0,'1.36. By killing these sons of Dhritarashtra, what pleasure can
be ours, O Janardana? Only sin will accrue to us from killing these felons.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.37',0,'1.37. Therefore, we should not kill the sons of Dhritarashtra,
our relatives; for how can we be happy by killing our own people, O Madhava
(Krishna)?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.38',0,'1.38. Though they, with intelligence overpowered by greed, see
no evil in the destruction of families, and no sin in hostility to friends,'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.39',0,'1.39. Why should not we who clearly see evil in the destruction
of families, learn to turn away from this sin, O Janardana (Krishna)?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.40',0,'1.40. In the destruction of a family, the immemorial religious
rites of that family perish; on the destruction of spirituality, impiety,
indeed, overcomes the whole family.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.41',0,'1.41. By the prevalence of impiety, O Krishna, the women of
the family become corrupt; and , women being corrupted, O Varshenya (descendant
of Vrishni), there arises intermingling of castes.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.42',0,'1.42. Confusion of castes leads to hell the slayers of the family,
for their forefathers fall, deprived of the offerings of rice-ball and
water (libations).'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.43',0,'1.43. By these evil deeds of the destroyers of the family, which
cause confusion of castes, the eternal religious rites of the caste and
the family are destroyed.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.44',0,'1.44. We have heard, O Janardana, that inevitable is the dwelling
for an unknown period in hell for those men in whose families the religious
practices have been destroyed.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.45',0,'1.45. Alas! We are involved in a great sin, in that we are
prepared to kill our kinsmen, through greed for the pleasures of a kingdom.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.46',0,'1.46. If the sons of Dhritarashtra with weapons in hand should
slay me in battle, unresisting and unarmed, that would be better for me.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.47',0,'1.47. Sanjaya said Having thus spoken in the midst of the battlefield, Arjuna,
casting away his bow and arrow, sat down on the seat of the chariot with
his mind overwhelmed with sorrow.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:1.48',0,'Swami Sivananda did not comment on this sloka'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.1',0,'2.1 Sanjaya said To him who was thus overcome with pity and who was despondent, with eyes full of tears and agitated, Madhusudana (the destroyer of Madhu) or Krishna spoke these words.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.2',0,'2.2 The Blessed Lord said Whence is this perilous strait come upon thee, this dejection which is unworthy of you, disgraceful, and which will close the gates of heaven upon you, O Arjuna?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.3',0,'2.3 Yield not to impotence, O Arjuna, son of Pritha. It does not befit thee. Cast off this mean weakness of the heart! Stand up, O scorcher of the foes!'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.4',0,'2.4 Arjuna said How, O Madhusudana, shall I fight in battle with arrows against Bhishma and Drona, who are fit to be worshipped, O destroyer of enemies?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.5',0,'2.5 Better it is, indeed, in this world to accept alms than to slay the most noble teachers. But if I kill them, even in this world all my enjoyments of wealth and fulfilled desires will be stained with (their) blood.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.6',0,'2.6 I can hardly tell which will be better, that we should coner them or that they should coner us. Even the sons of Dhritarashtra, after slaying whom we do not wish to live, stand facing us.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.7',0,'2.7 My heart is overpowered by the taint of pity; my mind is confused as to duty. I ask Thee: Tell me decisively what is good for me. I am Thy disciple. Instruct me who has taken refuge in Thee.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.8',0,'2.8 I do not see that it would remove this sorrow that burns up my senses, even if I should attain prosperous and unrivalled dominion on earth or lordship over the gods.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.9',0,'2.9 Sanjaya said Having spoken thus to Hrishikesha (the Lord of the senses), Arjuna (the coneror of sleep), the destroyer of foes, said to Krishna, "I will not fight" and became silent.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.10',0,'2.10 To him who was despondent in the midst of the two armies, Krishna, as if smiling, O Bharata, spoke these words.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.11',0,'2.11 The Blessed Lord said Thou hast grieved for those that should not be grieved for, yet thou speakest words of wisdom. The wise grieve neither for the living nor for the dead.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.12',0,'2.12 Nor at any time indeed was I not, nor thou, nor these rulers of men, nor verily shall we ever cease to be hereafter.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.13',0,'2.13 Just as in this body the embodied (soul) passes into childhood, youth and old age, so also does it pass into another body; the firm man does not grieve thereat.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.14',0,'2.14 The contacts of the senses with the objects, O son of Kunti, which cause heat and cold, pleasure and pain, have a beginning and an end; they are impermanent; endure them bravely, O Arjuna.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.15',0,'2.15 That firm man whom, surely, these afflict not, O chief among men, to whom pleasure and pain are the same, is fit for attaining immortality.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.16',0,'2.16 The unreal hath no being; there is non-being of the real; the truth about both has been seen by the knowers of the Truth (or the seers of the Essence).'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.17',0,'2.17 Know that to be indestructible, by Which all this is pervaded. None can cause the destruction of That, the Imperishable.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.18',0,'2.18 These bodies of the embodied Self, Which is eternal, indestructible and immeasurable, are said to have an end. Therefore fight, O Arjuna.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.19',0,'2.19 He who takes the Self to be the slayer and he who thinks It is slain, neither of them ï1knowsï1. It slays not, nor is It slain.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.20',0,'2.20 It is not born, nor does It ever die; after having been, It again ceases not to be; unborn, eternal, changeless and ancient, It is not killed when the body is killed.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.21',0,'2.21 Whosoever knows It to be indestructible, eternal, unborn and inexhaustible, how can that man slay, O Arjuna, or cause to be slain?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.22',0,'2.22 Just as a man casts off worn-out clothes and puts on new ones, so also the embodied Self casts off worn-out bodies and enters others which are new.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.23',0,'2.23 Weapons cut It not, fire burns It not, water wets It not, wind dries It not.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.24',0,'2.24 This Self cannot be cut, burnt, wetted, nor dried up. It is eternal, all-pervading, stable, immovable and ancient.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.25',0,'2.25 This (Self) is said to be unmanifested, unthinkable and unchangeable. Therefore, knowing This to be such, thou shouldst not grieve.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.26',0,'2.26 But even if thou thinkest of It as being constantly born and constantly dying, even then, O mighty-armed, thou shouldst not grieve.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.27',0,'2.27 For certain is death for the born, and certain is birth for the dead; therefore, over the inevitable thou shouldst not grieve.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.28',0,'2.28 Beings are unmanifested in their beginning, manifested in their middle state, O Arjuna, and unmanifested again in their end. What is there to grieve about?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.29',0,'2.29 One sees This (the Self) as a wonder; another speaks of It as a wonder; another hears of It as a wonder; yet having heard, none understands It at all.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.30',0,'2.30 This, the Indweller in the body of everyone, is ever indestructible, O Arjuna; therefore, thou shouldst not grieve for any creature.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.31',0,'2.31 Further, having regard to thy duty, shouldst not waver, for there is nothing higher for a Kshatriya than a righteous war.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.32',0,'2.32 Happy are the Kshatriyas, O Arjuna! who are called upon to fight in such a battle that comes of itself as an open door to heaven.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.33',0,'2.33 But if thou wilt not fight this righteous war, then having abandoned thine own duty and fame, thou shalt incur sin.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.34',0,'2.34 People, too, will recount thy everlasting dishonour; and to one who has been honoured, dishonour is worse than death.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.35',0,'2.35 The great car-warriors will think that thou hast withdrawn from the battle through fear; and thou wilt be lightly held by them who have thought much of thee.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.36',0,'2.36 Thy enemies also, cavilling at thy power, will speak many abusive words. What is more painful than this?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.37',0,'2.37 Slain, thou wilt obtain heaven; victorious, thou wilt enjoy the earth; therefore, stand up, O son of Kunti, resolved to fight.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.38',0,'2.38 Having made pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat the same, engage thou in battle for the sake of battle; thus thou shalt not incur sin.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.39',0,'2.39 This, which has been taught to thee, is wisdon concerning Sankhya. Now listen to wisdom concerning Yoga, endowed with which, O Arjuna, thou shalt cast off the bonds of action.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.40',0,'2.40 In this there is no loss of effort, nor is there any harm (production of contrary results or transgression). Even a little of this knowledge (even a little practice of this Yoga) protects one from great fear.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.41',0,'2.41 Here, O joy of the Kurus, there is but a single one-pointed determination; many-branched and endless are the thoughts of the irresolute.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.42',0,'2.42 Flowery speech is uttered by the unwise, taking pleasure in the eulogising words of the Vedas, O Arjuna, saying, "There is nothing else."'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.43',0,'2.43 Full of desires, having heaven as their goal, (they utter speech which is directed to ends) leading to new births as the result of their works, and prescribe various methods abounding in specific actions, for the attainment of pleasure and power.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.44',0,'2.44 For those who are attached to pleasure and power, whose minds are drawn away by such teaching, ï1thatï1 determinate reason is not formed which is steadily bent on meditation and Samadhi (superconscious state).'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.45',0,'2.45 The Vedas deal with the three attributes (of Nature); be thou above these three attributes. O Arjuna, free yourself from the pairs of opposites, and ever remain in the ality of Sattva (goodness), freed from (the thought of) acisition and preservation, and be established in the Self.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.46',0,'2.46 To the Brahmana who has known the Self, all the Vedas are of as much use as is a reservoir of water in a place where there is a flood.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.47',0,'2.47 Thy right is to work only, but never with its fruits; let not the fruits of action be thy motive, nor let thy attachment be to inaction.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.48',0,'2.48 Perform action, O Arjuna, being steadfast in Yoga, abandoning attachment and balanced in success and failure. Evenness of mind is called Yoga.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.49',0,'2.49 Far lower than the Yoga of wisdon is action, O Arjuna. Seek thou refuge in wisdom; wretched are they whose motive is the fruit.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.50',0,'2.50 Endowed with wisdom (evenness of mind), one casts off in this life both good and evil deeds; therefore, devote thyself to Yoga; Yoga is skill in action.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.51',0,'2.51 The wise, possessed of knowledge, having abandoned the fruits of their actions, and being freed from the fetters of birth, go to the place which is beyond all evil.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.52',0,'2.52 When thy intellect crosses beyond the mire of delusion, then thou shalt attain to indifference as to what has been heard and what has yet to be heard.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.53',0,'2.53 When thy intellect, which is perplexed by the Veda text, which thou hast read, shall stand immovable and steady in the Self, then thou shalt attain Self-realisation.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.54',0,'2.54 Arjuna said What, O Krishna, is the description of him who has steady wisdom, and is merged in the superconscious state? How does one of steady wisdom speak, how does he sit, how does he walk?'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.55',0,'2.55 The Blessed Lord said When a man completely casts off, O Arjuna, all the desires of the mind and is satisfied in the Self by the Self, then is he said to be one of steady wisdom.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.56',0,'2.56 He whose mind is not shaken by adversity, who does not hanker after pleasures, and is free from attachment, fear and anger, is called a sage of steady wisdom.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.57',0,'2.57 He who is everywhere without attachment, on meeting with anything good or bad, who neither rejoices not hastes, his wisdom is fixed.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.58',0,'2.58 When, like the tortoise which withdraws on all sides its limbs, he withdraws his senses from the sense-objects, then his wisdom becomes steady.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.59',0,'2.59 The objects of the senses turn away from the abstinent man leaving the longing (behind); but his longing also turns away on seeing the Supreme.'),
('swami-sivananda-et:source:2.60',0,'2.60 The turbulent senses, O Arjuna, do violently carry away the mind of a wise man though he be striving (to control them).');
